# GETENV(3)

## NAME

getenv - value for environment name

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

char *getenv(const char *name);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Getenv* searches the environment list (see environ(5)) for a string of the form
*name*\&**=**\&*value* and returns *value* if such a string is present, otherwise 0 (NULL).

## SEE ALSO

environ(5), exec(2), end(3)

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/getenv.c, lib/libc/csu/crt0.s

## BESM-6 NOTES

### Where environ comes from

Nothing is handed to a program in a register. Both gates - the kernel's *exece*() and b6sim's
loader - lay the argument block at the fixed base **070000** and zero every register but the
stack pointer, which is already seeded and must not be touched.

*Crt0* reads *argc* from that word and computes

> ```
> environ = &block[argc + 2]
> ```

\- past *argc* itself, the *argc* pointers, and the null that terminates the **argv[]** vector.
*Environ* is one word of bss defined there.

### Every slot strides by one word

A **char \*** is a fat pointer and carries a byte offset, but a **char \*\*** is a plain word
address - which is what *argv* and *envp* are here - so walking the vector is ordinary word
indexing and no decoding is involved.

### The environment is not a good thing to write a test against

lib/test/environ checks *getenv* against the vector it was handed, entry by entry, and prints
**neither a name nor a count.** Under b6sim the environment is a whitelisted slice of the
host's, so even MAKEFLAGS alone would make a count differ between a run under *make* and the
same run by hand. An expectation file may record only what the program does.
